    'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>India is one of the world's largest consumer markets, and foreign brands of cosmetics, eyewear, home decor, food, electronics and toys are all competing for a place on its shelves and marketplaces. But packaged goods cannot simply be shipped in with their global packaging. India's <strong>Legal Metrology (Packaged Commodities) Rules, 2011</strong> set strict rules for how packaged goods are declared, and they apply from the moment the goods are imported.</p>
<p>This guide is for <strong>manufacturers and brand owners outside India</strong>, and the Indian importers who work with them. It explains who needs the <strong>LMPC registration</strong>, what your packs must show, and how to avoid delays at Indian customs.</p>

<h2>Who Holds the LMPC Registration?</h2>
<p>Rule 27 of the rules requires every <strong>manufacturer, packer and importer</strong> of pre-packaged commodities in India to register with the Legal Metrology authorities. For goods made abroad, the registration is held by the <strong>business in India that imports them</strong>, typically:</p>
<ul>
<li>your <strong>importer or distributor</strong> in India, or</li>
<li>your own <strong>Indian subsidiary</strong>, if you import directly.</li>
</ul>
<p>If the goods are imported in bulk and packed into retail packs in India, the business doing the packing also needs <strong>packer registration</strong>. Either way, as the brand owner, it is in your interest to make sure your partner is registered before you ship, because a held shipment hurts your launch too. Read more about <a href="/resources/blogs/lmpc-certification-for-importers-in-india-why-it-is-mandatory-and-how-it-fits-into-overall-compliance">why LMPC is mandatory for importers</a>.</p>

<h2>Why Indian Customs Cares</h2>
<p>When packaged commodities arrive in India, <strong>customs checks that the importer complies with the Legal Metrology rules for packaged goods</strong>, and may ask for the <strong>LMPC certificate</strong>. If the registration is missing or the packs do not carry the required declarations, goods can be held until it is sorted out, adding port charges and delaying sales.</p>

<h2>What Your Packs Must Show in India</h2>
<p>The required declarations go on the principal display panel of each retail package:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Declaration</th><th>What it means for a foreign brand</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Name and address of the importer</td><td>Your Indian importer's details, not only your own</td></tr>
<tr><td>Country of origin</td><td>Where the product was made</td></tr>
<tr><td>Common or generic name</td><td>What the product is, not just the brand name</td></tr>
<tr><td>Net quantity</td><td>In standard metric units such as g, kg, ml or l</td></tr>
<tr><td>Month and year of manufacture or import</td><td>As required for the product</td></tr>
<tr><td>Maximum retail price (MRP)</td><td>In Indian rupees, inclusive of all taxes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Consumer care details</td><td>A contact in India for complaints</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Some products, such as food, cosmetics and electronics, also have their own sector-specific labelling rules on top of these.</p>

<h2>Global Packaging or India-Specific Packs?</h2>
<p>Global packaging rarely works for India as it is: it usually lacks an Indian importer address, a rupee MRP and Indian consumer care details. Brands generally choose one of two routes:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>India-specific packs</strong> printed at the factory. This is best for regular, larger volumes.</li>
<li><strong>Compliant stickers</strong> carrying the Indian declarations, applied to global packs before the goods are cleared and sold. This is good for smaller or new ranges.</li>
</ul>
<p>Either way, we review the artwork against the Indian rules before you print, so it is right the first time.</p>

<h2>State or Central Registration for Your Importer?</h2>
<p>Your importer can register with the <strong>state Legal Metrology department</strong> where it operates, or with the <strong>central Director of Legal Metrology</strong>, Department of Consumer Affairs, Government of India. <strong>Both are equally valid.</strong> A state registration can take additional time and may involve a physical audit of the premises. For importers selling across India, we recommend the central registration, which we make sure is issued on time, saving time, cost and renewal hassle. See <a href="/resources/blogs/central-vs-state-lmpc-registration-importers-packers">central vs state LMPC registration</a>.</p>

<h2>Other Indian Approvals to Plan For</h2>
<p>LMPC is often one of several approvals a foreign product needs for India. Depending on the product, you may also need:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>BIS certification</strong> for electronics, toys and many other notified products; see <a href="/resources/blogs/how-to-get-bis-certificate-for-import-in-india">BIS certification for imports</a>.</li>
<li><strong>EPR registration</strong> for plastic packaging, electronics or batteries; see <a href="/services/indian-registration-compliances/environment-compliance/epr-for-plastic-waste">EPR for plastic packaging</a>.</li>
<li>An <strong>Importer Exporter Code (IEC)</strong> for your importer; see <a href="/services/indian-registration-compliances/import-export/import-export-code-iec">IEC registration</a>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Planning them together avoids surprises after the goods have shipped.</p>

